                    <birth when="1000-01-01">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1796"/>
                    <occupation>Poet</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>See Ed Pope's notes: The first instance above must have been Robert Lovell
                            (DNB died 3.5.1796) the friend and brother-in-law of Coleridge and
                            Southey (M Ray Adams, Studies in the Literary Backgrounds of English
                            Radicalism, p133; Hazlitt's Works v.3 p.278). Mrs Lovel in 1801 at
                            Lamb's was almost certainly Robert Lovell's widow Mary nee Fricker. We have also included: The
                            final two instances of Lovel in 1822, which could refer to Robert Lovell's son
                            Robert who is said to have become a printer in London 1824 (DNB). Brides
                            Passage was next to the Fleet Street bookshop of Richard Carlile, who
                            was in prison in 1822.</p>
                    </note>
